  "summary": "An art movement that challenged traditional notions of art by incorporating everyday objects and popular culture.",
  "description": "Characterized by the use of found objects, collage, assemblage, and appropriation, often with humorous or satirical intent, reflecting the influence of mass media and consumer culture. Emerged as a reaction against the perceived seriousness of Abstract Expressionism, embracing the absurd and irreverent.",
  "culturalContext": "Neo-Dada (1955-62) — Jasper Johns's flags and targets, Robert Rauschenberg's Combines, Allan Kaprow's environments, Cage's circle — revived Duchampian irreverence inside Abstract Expressionism's hangover: everyday objects as paintings, bed quilts on canvas, chance operations as composition. The term (coined by Barbara Rose, 1958) bridged Pop and Fluxus; Neo-Dada's \"the gap between art and life\" remains the hinge of contemporary art's everyday-material logic.",
